// A circuit rule writes no `lhs ==> rhs` pair, because its **wiring**
// determines one. That is the whole point of the block form: a rewrite that
// happens inside a context is a diagram, and the diagram *is* the rewrite.
//
// Read the block below as a picture. `x` and `y` come in on the left; `p` is a
// rewrite the rule is parameterized by; the `node` lines say what is wired to
// what; `z` goes out on the right. Two boundary terms fall out of that one
// picture, by resolving the declared output port `z` twice:
//
//   source reading:  z |-> add(x′, y) |-> add(x, y)     // p replaced by its source
//   target reading:  z |-> add(x′, y) |-> add(x′, y)    // p replaced by its target
//
// so the rule is `add(x, y) ==> add(x′, y)`, and nobody wrote that down.
//
// Every arrow reports the kind of the thing it belongs to, and that redundancy
// is deliberate. `add(x′, y) --> (z)` carries the circuit former because `add`
// is an `oper`; `p(x) ==> (x′)` carries the rewrite face because `p` is a
// `rule`. So a reader sees redex against frame locally, and a disagreement is a
// named error rather than a silent reinterpretation.
//
// Wire names are the discipline: each is produced exactly once and consumed
// exactly once. `x′` is written in no port list, so it is an *internal wire* --
// implicit, computed by the name-set fold, never declared. Misspell it and you
// get a linearity failure, not a silent fresh name.
sign Nat {
  sort Nat : Type
  data Zero : Nat
  data Succ : Nat --> Nat
  oper add : (Nat, Nat) --> Nat

  rule cong1 : (
    rule p : Nat ==> Nat,
    data x : Nat,
    data y : Nat
  ) ==> (z : Nat) {
    node : p(x) ==> (x′);
    node : add(x′, y) --> (z);
  }
}

// What the description table holds for the block above, read back in the
// ruled surface spelling — the sign normal form of the signature
// unification: the declared sort, judgment-style data and oper members with
// the arrow grid, and the circuit member as a rule with its rewrite-sorted
// telescope and the sphere its wiring derived.
//@ mode: desc
//@ expect-desc-render: sign Nat { sort Nat : Type; data Zero : Nat; data Succ : Nat --> Nat; oper add : (Nat, Nat) --> Nat; rule cong1 : (rule p : Nat ==> Nat) add(x, y) ==> add(x′, y) }

// The cell layer admits it: two frame-defining cells (one per constructor) plus
// the rule's own cell, which entered through the same admission seam a written
// `rule` member passes.
//@ expect-desc-store-cells: 3

// And the block denotes one whiskered composite -- the redex sitting inside the
// frame, which is what a congruence *is*: the free coherence of parallel
// composition, with one side degenerate.
//@ expect-desc-composites: 1
